 "endCard": 71.8333,
 "end": {
  "title": "Next drop: why ice *floats*",
  "follow": "Follow Rest Frame",
  "foot": "Torricelli, 1644: the same air held a column of mercury about 76 cm high."
 },
 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 3.372,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "What lifts a drink up a straw? Something invisible, pushing."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 4.166666666666667,
   "dur": 7.376,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "A glass of water. A straw. One sip. The water climbs twenty centimeters, to your lips. It feels like you're pulling it."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 12.1,
   "dur": 6.268,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Now fill it completely. Seal it, airtight, with a lid. Then sip, however hard you like. Nothing rises."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 19.2,
   "dur": 14.32,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "In sixteen forty-four, Torricelli saw why. We live at the bottom of an ocean of air, and it presses on everything. A sip lowers the pressure inside the straw, and the air outside pushes the drink up. How hard can it push?"
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 36.03333333333333,
   "dur": 13.878,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Make the straw three stories tall. Your lungs alone? About one meter. Pumping with cheeks and tongue, one stunt reached seven. With a perfect pump, the water stops near ten meters. At the top, room-temperature water boils."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 53.266666666666666,
   "dur": 7.652,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "Measure it: the air above one square centimeter weighs just over a kilogram. So does ten point three meters of water."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 61.63333333333333,
